  "worked_solution": "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{4} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/assets/488788313bd32999611a520ec26e4ba6fd68a56e0333a7252a0faaf7107cad90.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(16-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16\\cdot y{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot (2-(-2))-(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =64-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
